#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Transparent TLS proxy with the Allowlist for one VM (ADR 0014).

Runs as its own system user on a socket systemd hands over (socket activation):
port 443 on the Host's address in the VM's isolated network, where the VM's resolver
sends every name, or a Unix socket for tests. Each connection starts with a TLS
ClientHello, whose server name (SNI) is the destination; the proxy resolves that name
itself and connects to it on port 443. What is not a TLS handshake, or names no
server, is refused. Destinations in the LAN or on localhost are refused even when an
allowed domain resolves there. Every connection is logged as an English line that
agentbox reads back, ALLOWED, BLOCKED, ALERT, CLOSED, ERROR or HELD, written with the
package's leaf module proxy_log (agentbox/proxy_log.py), which owns their format.
A refused connection gets a fatal TLS alert (access_denied for a blocked destination,
unrecognized_name without a server name, internal_error for an unreachable
destination), so the client reports why; bytes that are no TLS get nothing.

Allowlist: /etc/agentbox/allowlist.json (agentbox allow/disallow/...), entries with a
domain (covering its subdomains too), a scope ("all" or the name of a VM) and an optional
expiry (expires, epoch seconds). Expired entries allow no new connections. Changes apply
without a restart; a broken file, also one with a single broken or second entry (checked as
agentbox checks them, list_records.allowlist_entries), changes nothing: the last good state
stays, and a proxy that starts with one allows nothing until it is fixed. While the file does
not exist (before agentbox migrate-allowlist), the old lists /etc/agentbox/allowlist and
allowlist-<vm> apply, one valid domain per line.

A Regex entry (ADR 0013) has a pattern (regex) instead of the domain, compiled once
whenever the file is read again; one that breaks the pattern rules (list_records) is
left out.

While /etc/agentbox/open-<vm> holds a time in the future, every public domain is allowed
(Open web for a while) that no Forbid covers.

Forbids: /etc/agentbox/forbids.json (agentbox forbid/unforbid/...), entries as the Allowlist's,
a domain (covering its subdomains too) for one VM with an optional expiry. A destination a Forbid
of this VM covers and that no Allowlist entry allows is refused at once, never held, also in Open
web, and a tunnel only Open web allows closes when a Forbid comes to cover it:
  [<vm>] BLOCKED <host> (forbidden)
  [<vm>] CLOSED <host> (forbidden)
An Allowlist entry wins over a Forbid (agentbox keeps the two from overlapping: forbids.py).
Expired Forbids refuse nothing. Changes apply without a restart; a broken file keeps the last good
state, and a proxy that starts with one refuses at once whatever no Allowlist entry allows (no
hold, no Open web) until it is fixed.

Strong Signals always block (ADR 0021): every destination is classified on every connection,
and one with strong Signals is blocked whatever would allow it, a domain entry, a Regex entry
or Open web:
  [<vm>] BLOCKED <host> (suspicious: <signals>)
While the hand-maintained Watchlist cannot be read (watchlist.json broken, or missing without
the text file from before), which Signals are strong is unknown, so nothing passes at all;
without agentbox-suspicious, which tells them, neither:
  [<vm>] BLOCKED <host> (Watchlist unreadable)

Hold: a connection to a destination that nothing allows and no Forbid covers, that has no strong
Signal and is not on the Watchlist (no Signal of a Watchlist category) waits up to the hold time (the Setting
hold_seconds in /etc/agentbox/settings.json, read again when it changes; 0 refuses at once), so
the owner can allow it meanwhile. The proxy logs it once per host while connections to it are
held (at most MAX_ANNOUNCED new lines in HELD_WINDOW seconds: a VM trying endless names must not
drown the Control Room; further holds still hold, they only stay unannounced), the Control Room
notifies about it:
  [<vm>] HELD <host> (up to <seconds> s)
It decides again every check (CHECK_SECONDS): an entry added meanwhile, or Open web, lets the
connection through (ALLOWED), a strong Signal it gains meanwhile or a Watchlist that becomes
unreadable refuses it at once, and when the hold time runs out, the client leaves or sends more
than HOLD_EXTRA_LIMIT bytes (it only waits for the answer to its ClientHello) it is refused
as without a hold (BLOCKED not on the Allowlist); a Forbid added meanwhile refuses it at once. In
Open web nothing is held: it lets through whatever has no strong Signal and no Forbid. At most MAX_HELD connections are held at once; more are refused at
once. Holding costs the event loop nothing: each held connection sleeps between its checks.

Tunnels (ADR 0005): open tunnels run without a time limit, also once the entry that allowed
them expires. The proxy closes a tunnel when access to it ends on purpose and nothing else
allows it any more: an entry is removed before it expired (agentbox disallow, or a Forbid that
takes its place), Open web ends, or a Forbid comes to cover a tunnel only Open web allows. It also closes a tunnel whose destination gains a strong Signal (a Feed update, a new
Watchlist entry), after an Alert, and every tunnel while the Watchlist cannot be read
(ADR 0021): CLOSED <host> (suspicious: <signals>) or (Watchlist unreadable). It classifies its
open tunnels again only when a file the Signals come from changes (agentbox-suspicious
signals_key), not on every check.
"""
import asyncio
